## What this shows (2026-09-20)

Runtime alert delivery, proven end to end against the live deployment, and
two defects found by proving it rather than assuming it.

Method: five turns as `audit-physician` on AF-DQ-A2 carrying
`X-Copilot-Fault: tool:medications`, the same method as the 2026-09-18
rehearsal. Each returned `status=partial` with `medications=unavailable`.

**Defect 1 — the alert could not fire.** `/metrics` showed six tools, all
`ok` or `empty`, with no `medications` row at all. `_call_batch` resolves
fault-injected and invalid-params tools locally and `continue`d past the
`metrics.tool_call` increment, which the batched-gateway change (046b96e,
2026-09-19) left running only for tools that reach the gateway. The
tool-failure alert reads `copilot_tool_calls_total`, so its numerator was
structurally zero. The 2026-09-18 page predates batching, which is why the
alert looked healthy. `invalid_params` — a real production failure, the model
emitting a parameter the contract rejects — was equally invisible. Fixed in
`dbf5372` with a graph regression test.

**Defect 2 — delivery failed silently.** With the counter fixed the alert
fired correctly on both rules and reported `"webhook_delivered": false`.
Slack rejects any body without `text` or `blocks` as `invalid_payload`, and
the job posted the raw alert record. Fixed in `0471178`: the payload now
leads with a one-line summary and keeps every structured field after it, so
one body serves Slack and a generic receiver.

**Result.** Both rules fired and both delivered, `"webhook_delivered": true`,
and the two pages appeared in the Slack channel at 23:23 local:

    [PAGE] tool_failure_rate: Tool medications returned unavailable on
    100.00% of 5 calls; a service path is broken.
    [PAGE] tool_failure_rate: 5 of 35 clinical tool calls returned
    unavailable (14.29%); above the 5% page threshold.

The denominators are exact: 5 of 5 medications calls, 5 of 35 tool calls
across the five turns. The webhook URL is a file secret
(`/run/secrets/slack_alert_webhook`), never an argv value visible to
`docker inspect`, and is not in the repository.

## The other half: no alert during a real run (2026-09-20)

Pipeline 24351, job 79057, 761 s of the full live eval suite against the
deployment on the submission commit. Nothing fired.

That absence is evidence, not a gap. The staged test above shows the alert
pages when a tool genuinely fails; this shows it stays quiet through twelve
minutes of real workload. A threshold that never produces a false positive
under normal operation is the harder half to demonstrate, and both halves now
have a dated artifact behind them.

What the window actually contained: 12 `forbidden` denials from
`AUTH-FRONTDESK-001`, correctly excluded from the failure numerator because a
denial is the access control working; one `fault_injected` on `lab_results`
from `TOOL-OUTAGE-LABS-001`, correctly counted but far too small a share of
the window's tool calls to cross 5%; and a 5-minute p95 around 17.8 s against
a 30 s warn line.

Note for anyone reading the alert stream on this host: `fault_injected`
counts toward the tool-failure rate, so an eval run with several fault cases
in one window can legitimately page here. It cannot happen in production —
`fault_injection` defaults to `False` (`agent/app/settings.py`) — but on the
demo box it is expected behaviour rather than a misfire.
